# kickoff — generated by `kickoff adopt` (recorded in .kickoff/adopt-manifest.json; regenerated on `kickoff pull`).
# TRACKED on purpose (team-shareable): KICKOFF.md, KICKOFF.local.md, bin/, memory/ (except memory/private/),
# this file.
# Instance-private config + derived runtime never reach origin:
instance.env
# The PRIVATE half of the memory corpus. memory-retrieval/lib/memory.mjs indexes `<memory>/private/*.md`
# off the FILESYSTEM, not off git, so these facts are recalled locally and NEVER pushed — but only if
# this line exists. Without it the adopter's operator/instance specifics are untracked-but-stageable and
# the next `git add -A` sends them to their origin. memory/ above is team-shareable; memory/private/ is not.
memory/private/
adopt-manifest.json
core.lock
state/
supervisor.lock
supervisor.log*
supervisor.session.pid
announce.lock
announce.count
announce.last
crew-review.last
model-fallback
bridge-outages.log
active-item
checkpoints/
restart-history
auto-pickup-off
adopt-brains-pending
crew-plan.json
# Runtime state the ENGINE writes into .kickoff/ during normal operation. Every name below was
# untracked-but-STAGEABLE until now — the next `git add -A` in an adopter would have sent it to
# their origin, exactly as the memory/private/ note above warns. orphan-notified.json is new in
# core-v0.32 (the boot check's notify-once ledger) and is what surfaced the rest; the others are
# older debt found in the same sweep. A .gitignore line added LATER cannot untrack a file that has
# already been committed, which is why they ride the release that creates the first of them.
# auth.env and secret.env are the serious two: they carry a live OAuth token and a bot token
# respectively, both written 0600, so committing either publishes a credential.
orphan-notified.json
auth.env
secret.env
auth-heal.state
auth-escalated
bridge-escalated
hop-blocked
refresh-requested
backups/
